1443bd31b83d9674b14c2b39fdc69b7291d3991d33de8027815afac4720949"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-317158" :question/id #uuid "4aa08466-4853-50ed-bdb1-d0fc81eb9622" :question/math-academy-id "q-317158" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=6{x}^{4}{y}^{2}.$ Fill the table with appropriate sign of $f(x,y)$ as shown below.\n\n| $y>0$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/5fd85410238231152b6f905921f594af15293d6efc54b0b1ce558131a05bf6ee.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/e95107f16eb80a06cd172c30ec78992838f974a91f9f2fa17b7c8ac77125c438.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | ${x}^{4}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/071868e3f8708714a2a2b96dc4092aae32a4e4489d98df0b5062d8af6c624e01.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-317158/field-1" "q-317158/field-2" "q-317158/field-3" "q-317158/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-317158"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-121260"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/c44c38febc908fe5e941978bf44bab2e2cb4bd250765950d84f9294c9c4c9c4b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/209576cd78e9beb1c0f7a9bce8267163df1668a830ffc5c06918f8a726600fbe.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-7x$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/cf7fdbafdf968d3e6adfe9544a3b9e5e1736ce22fc808567f1d66fc80916534e.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-149611"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/c44c38febc908fe5e941978bf44bab2e2cb4bd250765950d84f9294c9c4c9c4b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/209576cd78e9beb1c0f7a9bce8267163df1668a830ffc5c06918f8a726600fbe.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | ${y}^{3}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n  \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/433e949488dc7d20c4fda4d508941da3a12363750b7bf58eada642b98f130eb2.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-121256/selection/29bfdbd2b4051071c328de3ed63b5c7d131f814e086717ca4e71cd429037c405" :answer/id #uuid "7a639ede-c800-5638-852e-b10f5d311830" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/dbdaec8f14733f7051f1e8b851e8a5b06a30406100b8739ed578d0cce654b41a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121256/selection/9974b8acdae061067bcf9e040aa6564fa1f6cbf74d6e4b6e2bcbfdca6014eb9d" :answer/id #uuid "713a8014-b814-53e7-a93c-8241b95fa106" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/9dc64d5d42ee8ddf56415ef23664e14a7a0be1e27db2dbde5f952dca360482d9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121256/selection/e88221aa9ff967b20f58eb8b776b22e7bfcadbe34826348a4adef5