n/math-academy-id "q-121256" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=-6x{y}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.  Since we have strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/2686a8ba9bdae16d92a184e7b35e856de2d031ce68be7f9262e4447ac3c74d04.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/8cc0734082fafe3628e1433f4c66c742858af51d95c8a068a4bd0025f0dc331a.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-6x$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)>0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/896b351f836578ae08786cd22f94a30fd4e8c2d718197cd08d8dbbb0694a0f36.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-121256/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "490982ac-6197-58eb-8d94-2ef28d659d50"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121256"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-121252"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-3x$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/3cfdf7d548c430f225a78fa6c4d915d69207f0a1c090d13cdd530c90865731ef.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-121262"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/c44c38febc908fe5e941978bf44bab2e2cb4bd250765950d84f9294c9c4c9c4b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/209576cd78e9beb1c0f7a9bce8267163df1668a830ffc5c06918f8a726600fbe.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{2}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/fc2a67dd63a482a37cf91c66c1b9fde01ea8c26ba0e3d6f7b8a62c112aae4bb0.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9419"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-3x$ | $y^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/743df5f5c9232346866a01aed9afa3d41fff0f61bf7812b7ae5db9a43efe59f4.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > 0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <                0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<         0$ | $x> 0$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9423"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-6x^2$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040514/assets/17dd9e10234b6a69123241a882e267d95d505d1944d7c31ef563f9ac06b990af.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
